# POP CoCo3 port — line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: cmd.exe cannot parse an LF-only .bat file. It reads
# the batch as one mangled line and every command fails with a truncated name
# ("'wasm' is not recognized"). build.bat is the CLAUDE.md §1 build contract,
# so its line endings are load-bearing, not cosmetic. Without this, a clone on
# a machine configured core.autocrlf=input silently produces an unrunnable
# build. Pinned here rather than relying on any developer's git config.

# Default: let git normalise text in the repo, check out native.
* text=auto

# Windows batch — MUST be CRLF in the working tree, always.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell + Lua the harness feeds to MAME — MUST be LF, always.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.lua   text eol=lf

# ★★ ORACLE INSTRUMENTATION PATCHES — MUST be LF, always. coco_agi addition (P0.2); POP has
# no .patch files, so this EXTENDS the policy rather than diverging from it.
#
# WHY: `git apply` matches context lines BYTE FOR BYTE. A patch checked out CRLF under the
# `* text=auto` rule above, applied to ScummVM's LF tree, fails with "patch does not apply" on
# every hunk — MEASURED at P0.2, not assumed. That would break oracle/patches/ for anyone
# cloning fresh, which is precisely the reproducibility AC-10 exists to guarantee: pin + patch
# or it is not pinned (CLAUDE.md §2Q). Exactly the same class of load-bearing line-ending
# failure as the *.bat rule above, pointing the other way.
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff  text eol=lf

# 6809 assembly — LF (lwasm accepts either; keep diffs clean).
*.s     text eol=lf

# Binary / fixture: never touch.
*.bin   binary
*.dsk   binary
*.dmk   binary
*.hdv   binary
*.png   binary
*.pdf   binary
